<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Custoza (1848)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italian-war-of-independence">First Italian War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Custoza was fought on 24–25 July 1848 near Verona during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italian-war-of-independence">First Italian War of Independence</a>, between the Austrian army of Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-radetzky-von-radetz">Joseph Radetzky</a> and the Sardinian army of King Charles Albert. The Austrian victory forced the Piedmontese to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and accept the armistice of Salasco. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> reoccupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> held out until August 1849.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-custoza-1848.jpg" alt="Battle of Custoza (1848)" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felice Cerruti Bauduc (1817-1896) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sommacampagna_1848.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In March 1848 the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> rose against Austrian occupation. Charles Albert of Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> supported the Milanese revolt and declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> declared its independence from Austria. The Austrian Field Marshal Radetzky withdrew his forces from Milan to defensive positions based on the four fortresses known as the Quadrilateral: Verona, Mantua, Peschiera and Legnago. The Piedmontese took Peschiera after a short siege, but Radetzky received substantial reinforcements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>By July the Piedmontese army was widely dispersed, from the Rivoli plateau in the north to Governolo in the south. On 23 July Radetzky attacked the Piedmontese II Corps under General Ettore Gerbaix De Sonnaz and forced it back first before Peschiera and, after a second attack on the 24th, behind the River Mincio, which split the Piedmontese army in two.</p><p>The Piedmontese high command reacted slowly and uncertainly. It decided to attack the Austrian rear towards the village of Staffalo with the bulk of I Corps under General Eusebio Bava. The attack began on the afternoon of the 24th and succeeded, forcing back the single brigade covering the area. This lulled the Sardinian commanders into complacency and led Radetzky to halt his advance beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mincio">Mincio</a> and turn on these forces.</p><p>For the 25th the Piedmontese were ordered to attack the enemy further on, with II Corps to support from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mincio">Mincio</a>, but De Sonnaz refused to obey the order, claiming his troops were too tired. What was meant to be an offensive became a struggle to hold the advancing enemy. All day the outnumbered Piedmontese faced attacks by two Austrian army corps, and by evening the whole line had been pushed back, though in good order and with the men still fighting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The battle was not a total victory for the Austrians: they suffered higher losses, and all major Piedmontese units kept their cohesion and equipment. It was nonetheless a considerable reversal. On the 26th the Piedmontese failed to stop the Austrians crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mincio">Mincio</a> at Volta Mantovana and suffered a decisive defeat. They fell back first behind the River Oglio and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. After a small battle on the outskirts of the city an armistice was signed, and the army retreated into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. An attempt to renew the war the next year brought another victory for Radetzky and effectively ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italian-war-of-independence">First Italian War of Independence</a>. Johann Strauss I's Radetzky March was commissioned to commemorate his victories.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Custoza (1848)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Risorgimento the army, including the Bersaglieri light infantry created in 1836, was beaten by Austria at <b>Custoza</b> in 1848 and Novara in 1849.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Piedmontese defeat at <b>Custoza</b> in July 1848, Radetzky reoccupied Milan on 6 August, and Lombardy returned to the Kingdom of Lombardy-Venetia.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-milan">Provisional Government of Milan</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic briefly voted in July 1848 to join the Kingdom of Sardinia, but after Piedmont's defeat at <b>Custoza</b> it returned to independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-san-marco">Republic of San Marco</a></li></ul></section>
